# Part 1: `.hql/config.toml`, the complete key set

- **Depends on**: [[HQL-0059]] (projects, the HQL home, packages at two
  levels), [[HQL-0025]] (layering and named contexts)

## Scope

Every key an HQL configuration file takes, which files take it, and how two
files that state it combine. Three files share one format:

| File | Level | Found at |
| --- | --- | --- |
| the project file | project | `<project>/.hql/config.toml`, by the walk or `--project` |
| the user file | home | `$HQL_HOME/config.toml`, else `~/.hql/config.toml` |
| the system file | system | `$HQL_SYSTEM_CONFIG`, else `/etc/hql/config.toml` (`%ProgramData%\hql\config.toml` on Windows) |

A file that is absent contributes nothing. A file that is present and
malformed, or that holds a key this part does not name, fails every run,
naming the file and the key. HyperMarkdown's `.hmd/config.toml` is not an
HQL configuration file and takes none of these keys.

## Keys

| Key | Project | User, system | Named context | Combines |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `[packages] dependencies` | yes | yes | no | per level: each level installs its own |
| `[packages] paths` | yes | yes | yes | gathered, each crate once |
| `[modules] roots` | yes | yes | yes | gathered, each directory once |
| `[modules] import` | yes | yes | yes | gathered, each module once |
| `[modules] prelude` | yes | yes | yes | the last layer that states it |
| `[execution] backend` | yes | yes | yes | ranked, one wins |
| `[execution] max_elements`, `max_bytes` | yes | no | no | project only |
| `[report] mode`, `level` | yes | yes | yes | ranked, one wins |
| `env` | yes | no | no | project only |
| `[endpoint.<id>]` | yes | no | no | project only |
| `[keyvault.<id>]` | yes | no | no | project only |
| `[format.<Type>]` | yes | no | no | project only |
| `current-context` | no | yes | no | the user file's, else the system file's |
| `[context.<name>]` | no | yes | no | the user file's replaces the system file's of one name |

**Gathered** keys collect in the layer order system, user, named context,
the home's `modules/`, project, `$HQL_PATH`, command line. **Ranked** keys
resolve as the command line, the project file, the environment variable,
the named context, the user file, the system file, then the default, as
HQL-0045 orders them. A relative path resolves against the project root in
the project file, and against the directory holding the file in the user
and system files.

## `[packages]`

The package dependencies of a level ([[HQL-0057]]).

```toml
[packages]
dependencies = ["ml"]          # installed packages, by name; no version
paths = ["../hql-ext-geo"]     # crates on disk, built on first use
```

- `dependencies` names packages installed into the same level's
  `packages/`; `lock.toml` beside the file pins each to one version and
  source. A version written here is refused: `lock.toml` is the one record
  of versions. A declared dependency that is not installed fails the run,
  naming `hql package install`.
- `paths` names crates on disk. Each is built under its own `target/` on
  first use and whenever its sources are newer than the library, and is
  never pinned.
- The builtin packages `std` and `hmd` are never named here.

## `[modules]`

Where modules come from, and which every unit imports implicitly
([[HQL-0024]], parts "Imports" and "The standard library").

```toml
[modules]
roots = ["lib"]                # module roots of this project's HQL sources
import = ["hmd", "ml"]         # imported by every unit, as a prelude line is
prelude = true                 # false withholds every prelude's imports
```

- `roots` names directories holding HQL modules, read as `lib/std/` is.
  `$HQL_PATH` and `--modules` add to them.
- `import` names modules. Each is imported into every unit of the run as
  a line of a prelude is, under its final segment. Importing a module of a
  package activates that package, so its `prelude.hql` applies too:
  `import = ["hmd"]` is how a project makes HyperMarkdown available to
  every unit. `--import <module>` is the command-line layer of this key.
- `prelude = false` keeps every prelude's imports, `std`'s and each active
  package's, out of the implicit list. The preludes still run.

## `[execution]`

How a checked program runs ([[HQL-0004]], [[HQL-0012]]).

```toml
[execution]
backend = "localhost"          # the execution backend; --backend, $HQL_BACKEND
max_elements = 1000000         # bound on the elements an endpoint read yields
max_bytes = 104857600          # bound on the bytes an endpoint read takes
```

- `backend` names a backend the binary has; an unknown name fails the run,
  naming the known ones.
- `max_elements` and `max_bytes` are counts. Each bounds every configured
  endpoint of the project.

## `[report]`

How much a run continues past a failure, and what reaches stderr
([[HQL-0045]]).

```toml
[report]
mode = "collect"               # strict | collect; --report, $HQL_REPORT
level = "info"                 # debug | info | warning | error; --report-level, $HQL_REPORT_LEVEL
```

## `env`

The environment variables a program may read through `io.env`
([[HQL-0041]], part "The environment").

```toml
env = ["HOME", "AWS_*"]        # names, and PREFIX_* patterns
```

- Absent, a run grants every variable. Stated, it grants the names and
  patterns it lists and no other. A document rendered by a host is granted
  what the host's policy grants.
- `env` is a top-level key, so it is written before the first table.

## `[endpoint.<id>]`

A configured endpoint, reached as `source("id")`, `target("id")`,
`tap("id")` or `sink("id")` ([[HQL-0012]], [[HQL-0021]], [[HQL-0035]],
[[HQL-0046]], [[HQL-0049]]).

```toml
[endpoint.users]
kind = "csv"
path = "data/users.csv"
element = "UserRow"
mode = "replace"
strictness = "compatible"

[endpoint.users.fields.id]
name = "user_id"
type = "INTEGER"
```

- The id matches `[a-z][a-z0-9_]*`. `vault` is not an id.
- Every endpoint takes `kind` and `element` (the HQL type of one element).
  Every kind but `vault` also takes `mode`, `strictness` and `fields`.
- A secret is never written: it is named by a key ending in `_env` that
  holds the name of an environment variable. A key named `password`,
  `token`, `secret` or `api_key` holding a literal is refused.

| `kind` | Keys |
| --- | --- |
| `csv`, `json`, `json_lines`, `yaml` | `path` |
| `sqlite` | `path`, `table`, `order` |
| `postgres` | `dsn_env`, `table`, `order` |
| `kafka` | `brokers` or `brokers_env`, `topic`, `from`, `idle_ms` |
| `nats` | `url` or `url_env`, `subject`, `idle_ms` |
| `nats_jetstream` | `url` or `url_env`, `stream`, `subject`, `from`, `idle_ms`, `consumer` |
| `spark` | `url` or `url_env`, `token_env`, `table`, `order` |
| `vault` | `root`; provided by `hmd`, and refused unless `hmd` is active |

| Key | Value |
| --- | --- |
| `mode` | `replace` (default), `append` or `create`: what a write does to existing data |
| `strictness` | `compatible` (default), `strict`, `open` or `warn`: how the boundary meets the external schema |
| `order` | a list of column names: the order a keyed read yields |
| `from` | `earliest` (default) or `latest`: where a stream read starts |
| `idle_ms` | milliseconds above 0: how long a stream read waits before it ends |
| `fields.<field>` | the schema boundary of one HQL field: `name` or `path`, `type`, `position`, `read`, `write`, `absent`, `lossy`, and a subtable named by the kind for keys the kind owns |

## `[keyvault.<id>]`

A store of named keys, reached through `crypto.keyvault` ([[HQL-0054]]).
Accepted, not built.

```toml
[keyvault.team]
kind = "file"
path = "keys.age"
passphrase_env = "HQL_TEAM_VAULT_PASSPHRASE"
```

| `kind` | Keys |
| --- | --- |
| `file` | `path`; `passphrase_env` or `identity_file` |
| `keychain` | `service`, default `hql.keyvault.<id>` |
| `ssh_agent` | `socket_env`, default `SSH_AUTH_SOCK` |
| `aws_kms` | `region` or `region_env`; `endpoint_url` |

`gcp_kms`, `azure_key_vault` and `vault_transit` are reserved and refused.
A key named `passphrase`, `secret`, `private_key`, `token` or
`secret_access_key` is refused, naming its `*_env` replacement.

## `[format.<Type>]`

The renderer a type is written with, per presentation format
([[HQL-0017]]).

```toml
[format.Graph]
markdown = "mermaid"
```

A table per type maps a format to a renderer. An unknown format or
renderer, or a renderer that does not write that format or that shape, is
refused when the file loads.

## Named contexts

Reusable environments, defined in the user and system files only
([[HQL-0025]]).

```toml
current-context = "work"

[context.work]
project = "~/code/acme"        # optional: binds a project
[context.work.modules]
roots = ["~/lib/acme-hql"]
[context.work.execution]
backend = "localhost"
```

- A context takes `project` and the keys the Keys table marks for it.
- Selection is `--context`, then `$HQL_CONTEXT`, then `current-context`,
  then a context named `default`. A selected name that no file defines
  fails the run.
- `project` is read against the declaring file. A context selected by
  `--context` or `$HQL_CONTEXT` whose project contradicts `--project` or
  `--bare` is an error; under `current-context`, the command line wins.

## Not HQL configuration

- `[semantics]`: the semantic index and its embedder are HyperMarkdown's,
  in `.hmd/config.toml` ([[HQL-0059]], "The semantic index is
  HyperMarkdown's").
- `[extensions]`: its two keys are `[modules] import` and
  `[modules] prelude`. A file stating `[extensions]` is refused, naming
  the `[modules]` key that replaces each.
- `[sources]`: refused, naming `[endpoint.<id>]`.
- `workspace`, in a named context: refused, naming `project`.

## Environment variables

| Variable | Meaning |
| --- | --- |
| `HQL_HOME` | the HQL home, else `~/.hql` |
| `HQL_SYSTEM_CONFIG` | the system file |
| `HQL_PATH` | module roots, added after the project's |
| `HQL_CONTEXT` | the named context of one run |
| `HQL_BACKEND` | `[execution] backend` |
| `HQL_REPORT`, `HQL_REPORT_LEVEL` | `[report] mode` and `level` |
| `HQL_REGISTRY`, `HQL_REGISTRY_REPOSITORY` | another registry, another repository of official packages ([[HQL-0057]]) |
| `HQL_SOURCE` | the `hql` checkout a package builds against |
